      "The absolute version carries the information a person needs. Risk rose from four in ten thousand to eight in ten thousand is less striking, harder to put in a headline, and the only form in which the size of the effect appears.",
      "Both numbers are honest and they are used for different reasons. A relative risk is the right quantity for comparing studies, because it stays stable across populations with different baseline rates, and that is why researchers report it.",
      "A third form is more useful again for a decision. The number needed to treat says how many people must take a medicine for one of them to benefit, which converts a probability into a count of actual people.",
      "There is also a difference between risk and rate. A risk is a probability over a stated period, so a figure given without its period is incomplete in a way that is easy to miss, since the sentence still reads as though it is finished.",
      "Attributable fraction is another term doing quiet work. It describes how much of the disease in a population would not occur without the exposure, which is a question about the population and not about any individual.",
