      "Its value is that it was written before the outcome was known. A log cannot be shaped by how the expedition turned out, and a day that later proved decisive is recorded with no more emphasis than any other.",
      "The second is the published narrative, written afterwards for a general audience. It has a shape, with a beginning, a crisis and a resolution, and the events are selected and ordered to produce that shape.",
      "That narrative is not less true and it is differently organised. Days that produced nothing are compressed into a sentence, and an incident that reads as a turning point may have been one of several similar incidents in the log.",
      "The third record is the data: specimens, measurements, photographs, samples. It emphasises what the expedition was for, and it is almost silent about the experience of obtaining any of it.",
      "Each medium therefore emphasises a different thing. The log emphasises sequence, the narrative emphasises meaning, and the data emphasise result, and no two of them disagree about any fact.",
      "The useful exercise is to take one day and read it in all three. The day will have a position and a weather note in the log, two paragraphs of significance in the narrative, and three specimen labels in the collection.",
