      "The difference is whether the revision was principled or convenient. A principled revision is derived from a reason independent of the failure, and it makes a new prediction that could itself fail, which the original claim no longer can.",
      "A convenient revision does neither. It adds exactly the condition needed to explain away the result just obtained, it is introduced only afterwards, and it makes no prediction that any future test could disappoint.",
      "A claim that has been rescued this way several times reaches a particular state. It is now compatible with every possible observation, which its holder may describe as its strength and which has made it impossible to learn anything from.",
      "The pattern is easiest to see in the history of a dispute rather than at any one moment. Any single revision looks defensible; five of them in sequence, each triggered by a result rather than by an argument, is the whole phenomenon.",
