    t: "The Map of the Ocean Floor",
    std: 10, hi: 1240, lo: 1020,
    H: [
      "For most of history the sea floor was assumed to be a featureless plain. Nothing contradicted the assumption, because nobody could see it and the few available soundings were taken by lowering a weighted line.",
      "Echo sounding changed what was available. A ship could record the depth continuously along its track, and by the late 1940s there were thousands of such tracks, each a profile of the bottom along one line.",
      "Marie Tharp worked at Lamont, in New York, converting those profiles into maps. She was not permitted on the research vessels for most of the 1950s, so she worked from data collected by colleagues who were.",
      "The method was laborious and the result was new. By plotting profiles against each other she could draw the sea floor as a landscape, with mountains, escarpments and valleys rendered as a physiographic map a non-specialist could read.",
      "What appeared in the middle of the Atlantic was not a plain. There was a continuous mountain range, and running along the crest of it was a deep valley, a rift, which she identified from the profiles crossing it.",
      "Her colleague Bruce Heezen initially dismissed the rift, reportedly because it supported continental drift, which was then a disreputable idea. The valley implied the sea floor was being pulled apart, which meant continents moved.",
      "The confirmation came from a different dataset. Earthquake epicentres in the Atlantic, plotted independently, fell along a line, and when the two maps were compared the line of earthquakes lay inside the rift she had drawn.",
      "The map did something an argument could not. A physiographic map is readable by anyone, and a feature that had been a disputed interpretation of numbers became a thing a person could point at on a wall.",
    ],
